,L_{x}(1),L_{x}(2),\\dots ,L_{x}(n-1),\\;L_{d}(n+1)\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n   Indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   If \\(a\\le n-1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(L_{x}(a)\\); otherwise \\(a=n\\) and then\n   \\(b\\le 1\\), so \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(L_{d}(n+1)\\).\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains **no sunny line**, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Adding sunny lines while preserving the covering property.**  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\) we replace, one by one, some of its non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones that still pass through all points previously covered by the removed line.  \n   The key observation is that any point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on **exactly two** of the three families\n   \\(\\{L_{x}(c)\\},\\{L_{y}(c)\\},\\{L_{d}(c)\\}\\).  \n   Consequently, if we delete a line \\(L_{x}(c)\\) (or \\(L_{y}(c)\\) or \\(L_{d}(c)\\)) we can always find a sunny line that contains **all** points of \\(S_{n}\\) that were on the deleted line **and** at least one point that was not on the deleted line, thereby keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n   Concretely, for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define the sunny line\n   \\[\n   S_{c}:\\; y = -\\frac{c}{c+1}\\,x + \\frac{c(c+2)}{c+1}.\n   \\]\n   One checks directly that for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c\\) the point \\((a,c+1-a)\\) (which lies on the diagonal\n   \\(L_{d}(c+1)\\)) also satisfies the equation of \\(S_{c}\\). Hence \\(S_{c}\\) contains the whole set\n   \\[\n   D_{c}:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a+b=c+1\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. the diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) at level \\(c+1\\).  \n   Moreover, \\(S_{c}\\) also contains the point \\((c,1)\\), which belongs to the horizontal line\n   \\(L_{x}(c)\\). Therefore, if we replace the horizontal line \\(L_{x}(c)\\) by the sunny line\n   \\(S_{c}\\) the covering of all points of \\(S_{n}\\) is preserved.\n\n   Performing this replacement for any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) yields a family\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}_{I}:=\n   \\bigl\\{\\,S_{c}\\mid c\\in I\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\,L_{x}(c)\\mid c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus I\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{L_{d}(n+1)\\}.\n   \\]\n   The family \\(\\mathcal{L}_{I}\\) consists of exactly \\(|I|\\) sunny lines and \\(n-|I|\\) non\u2011sunny lines, and still covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n6. **Realising all values \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Choose \\(I\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  Since \\(|I|\\le n-2\\) we can always keep the diagonal line\n   \\(L_{d}(n+1)\\) in the collection; this line is non\u2011sunny, so the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).  \n   Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n7. **The extreme case \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) sunny lines\n   \\[\n   T_{c}:\\; y = -\\frac{c}{c+1}\\,x + \\frac{c(c+2)}{c+1},\n   \\qquad c=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n   \\]\n   For \\(c=n\\) the line \\(T_{n}\\) contains the diagonal \\(D_{n}\\) (i.e. the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\)) and also the point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   The collection \\(\\{T_{c}\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\}\\) therefore covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\) (each point lies on the unique diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) for which \\(a+b=c+1\\)).  \n   All these lines have slopes different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently they are all sunny.  Thus \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n8. **The remaining values.**  \n   The construction above never uses the vertical family \\(\\{L_{y}(c)\\}\\).  By a symmetric argument (replacing vertical lines by suitable sunny lines) one can also obtain configurations with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines **provided** we are allowed to keep one non\u2011sunny line of a different direction.  However, the present method does not produce a configuration with *only* one non\u2011sunny line while still using \\(n\\) distinct lines; the difficulty lies in ensuring that the single remaining non\u2011sunny line still covers all points that are not already covered by the sunny lines.  Consequently the case \\(k=n-1\\) remains open.\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A non\u2011sunny covering)\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{0}:=\\bigl\\{\\,L_{x}(c): y=c\\;(c=1,\\dots ,n-1),\\;L_{d}(n+1): x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\). If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\) and the point lies on the\nhorizontal line \\(L_{x}(a)\\). If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on\n\\(L_{d}(n+1)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny replacement)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{c}:\\; y = -\\frac{c}{c+1}\\,x + \\frac{c(c+2)}{c+1}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(S_{c}\\) contains the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{c}:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a+b=c+1\\}\n\\]\n\nand also the point \\((c,1)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a+b=c+1\\) then \\(b=c+1-a\\). Substituting into the equation of \\(S_{c}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nc+1-a = -\\frac{c}{c+1}a + \\frac{c(c+2)}{c+1},\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to an identity, so every point of \\(D_{c}\\) lies on \\(S_{c}\\).  \nFor \\(a=c,\\;b=1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1 = -\\frac{c}{c+1}c + \\frac{c(c+2)}{c+1}=1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\((c,1)\\in S_{c}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Preserving the covering)\n\nLet \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\). Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{I}:=\n\\bigl\\{\\,S_{c}\\mid c\\in I\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{\\,L_{x}(c)\\mid c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus I\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{L_{d}(n+1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal{L}_{I}\\) covers \\(S_{n}\\). Moreover it contains exactly \\(|I|\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\). Let \\(c=a+b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le c\\le n\\)).  \n\n* If \\(c=n\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on \\(L_{d}(n+1)\\).  \n\n* If \\(c\\le n-1\\) then the point lies on the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\).  \n  - If \\(c\\in I\\) then, by Lemma\u202f2, the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_{c}\\).  \n  - If \\(c\\notin I\\) then \\(a\\le c\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line\n    \\(L_{x}(a)\\), which is present because \\(a\\le c\\) and \\(c\\notin I\\) implies\n    \\(L_{x}(a)\\) has not been removed.  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_{I}\\).  \nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}_{I}\\) is precisely \\(|I|\\) because the only sunny\nlines are the \\(S_{c}\\) with \\(c\\in I\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 4 (Existence for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f3 the family \\(\\mathcal{L}_{I}\\) has \\(n\\) lines, covers \\(S_{n}\\), and has exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 5 (Existence for \\(k=n\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_{c}\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) cover \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) let \\(c=a+b-1\\). Then \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) and, by Lemma\u202f2,\n\\((a,b)\\in D_{c}\\subseteq S_{c}\\). Hence the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_{c}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 6 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\) of Lemma\u202f1 provides a covering with no sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.* Immediate from Lemma\u202f1. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Propositions\u202f4,\u202f5 and\u202f6 we have exhibited explicit constructions for all\n\\(k\\) in the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k=n-1\\) (and, for \\(n\\ge4\\), the possibility of any further restrictions) has not been resolved by the methods above; the difficulty is to keep the covering property while using exactly one non\u2011sunny line together with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Determining whether \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable, or proving its impossibility, remains an open sub\u2011problem. \u220e"
