            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\); its cardinality is \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) are distinct and none of them is sunny because each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). By definition \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\). Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nThus a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  No configuration can contain a sunny line\n\nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nIn particular a sunny line is **not** parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. it is not vertical.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a non‑vertical line. For each integer \\(i\\) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap\\{x=i\\}\\) contains at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with finite slope \\(m\\). Substituting \\(x=i\\) yields the unique point \\((i,mi+b)\\). ∎\n\n---\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the **column**\n\n\\[\nC_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nThe column \\(C_i\\) contains at least one lattice point (namely \\((i,1)\\)).  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a valid configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines contains a sunny line \\(\\ell\\). By the lemma, \\(\\ell\\) meets each column \\(C_i\\) in at most one point, so \\(\\ell\\) cannot cover the whole column \\(C_i\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must lie on some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), each column \\(C_i\\) must be completely covered by the lines that intersect it. The only line capable of covering **all** points of \\(C_i\\) is the vertical line \\(x=i\\); any non‑vertical line contributes at most a single point of that column. Consequently the vertical line \\(x=i\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\) for every \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) already contains the \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\). There is no remaining slot for an additional line, contradicting the assumption that a sunny line is present. Hence a configuration satisfying the problem’s conditions cannot contain any sunny line.\n\nTherefore the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is forced to be  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited a construction achieving \\(k=0\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), and we have proved that any configuration meeting the covering requirement must consist solely of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); consequently no sunny line can appear. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
