om the definition of \\(T_n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   b_1\\le n+1-a,\\qquad b_2\\le n+1-c,\\qquad b_3\\le n+1-e .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two equalities in (2) and using (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m(c-a)+m(e-c)=b_2-b_1+b_3-b_2=b_3-b_1\\le (n+1-e)-(n+1-a)=a-e<0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(c-a>0\\) and \\(e-c>0\\), the left‑hand side is \\(m\\) multiplied by a\n   positive number; consequently \\(m<0\\).  \n   But \\(m\\neq-1\\) by the definition of a sunny line, so \\(m\\le -2\\).  \n   Using \\(m\\le-2\\) in the first equality of (2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1=m(c-a)\\le -2(c-a)\\le -2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(b_2\\le b_1-2\\).  Repeating the same argument with the second\n   equality of (2) yields \\(b_3\\le b_2-2\\le b_1-4\\).  \n   However, all three \\(b_i\\) are positive integers, so we must have\n   \\(b_1\\ge5\\).  Then from (3),\n\n   \\[\n   a\\le n+1-b_1\\le n-4 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(c>a\\), we get \\(c\\le n-3\\); similarly\n   \\(e\\le n-2\\).  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b_1\\le (n-4)+5=n+1,\\qquad\n   c+b_2\\le (n-3)+(b_1-2)=n+1,\\qquad\n   e+b_3\\le (n-2)+(b_1-4)=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   All three inequalities are actually equalities, which forces\n   \\(b_1=5,\\;b_2=3,\\;b_3=1\\) and \\(a=n-4,\\;c=n-3,\\;e=n-2\\).  \n   Substituting these values into the line equation \\(y=mx+b\\) gives\n   \\(m=-2\\) and \\(b= n+3\\).  The point \\((n-2,1)\\) then satisfies\n   \\(1=-2(n-2)+n+3\\), i.e. \\(1=n-1\\), which is impossible for \\(n\\ge3\\).\n   Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\); the maximal\n   number is two. ∎  \n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines**  \n\n   Let a configuration consist of \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(n-s\\) non‑sunny\n   lines (the latter are vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   By Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2s\\) points of\n   \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Every non‑sunny line can be taken to be vertical without loss of\n   generality: a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets at most one point in each\n   column, while a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets at most one point in each\n   column as well; replacing it by the vertical line that passes through\n   any of its points does not decrease the set of covered points.\n   Thus we may assume the \\(n-s\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines\n   \\(x=i_1,\\dots ,x=i_{\\,n-s}\\) for distinct indices \\(i_j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   The vertical line \\(x=i\\) covers exactly the whole column \\(C_i\\),\n   i.e. \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\) points.  Hence the total number of points covered\n   by the configuration is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{\\sum_{j=1}^{\\,n-s}(n+1-i_j)}_{\\text{vertical lines}}\n   \\;+\\;2s .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The sum in (4) is maximised when the omitted columns are the smallest\n   ones, i.e. when the indices \\(i_j\\) are the largest \\(n-s\\) numbers.\n   Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{\\,n-s}(n+1-i_j)\\le\n   \\sum_{i=s+1}^{n}(n+1-i)\n   =\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (4) and (5) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of\n   points that can be covered by any configuration with \\(s\\) sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   N(s)\\le \\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}+2s . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   To cover **all** points of \\(T_n\\) we need \\(N(s)\\ge |T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n   Substituting (6) and simplifying gives\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}{2}+2s\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   s\\,(n-3)\\le 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(n\\ge3\\), the inequality forces \\(s\\le 1\\).  Hence **at most\n   one sunny line can appear in a feasible configuration**.  Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Realisation of the admissible values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so it lies on\n   \\(L_a\\).  All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny.\n   Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (they cover all columns\n   except the last one) and add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y = x-n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to\n   the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)),\n   so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  It passes through the unique point of the last\n   column, namely \\((n,1)\\), and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  Together\n   the \\(n\\) lines cover every element of \\(T_n\\), and exactly one of them\n   (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every\n   \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   By (7) no configuration can contain two or more sunny lines, while the\n   explicit constructions above show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are\n   attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n   \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
