"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved a complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, at most \\(n-\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, and every integer in this interval can be attained.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(\\displaystyle |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.\n\n2. **How many points can a line contain?**  \n   * Lemma\u202f1: a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (a *non\u2011sunny* line) meets \\(S\\) in at most \\(n\\) points; the bound is attained by \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   * Lemma\u202f2: a sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most two points.  The proof uses the fact that three points of \\(S\\) on a line would force the line to have slope \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or be vertical, contradicting the definition of \u201csunny\u201d.\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   If exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, Lemma\u202f2 gives at most \\(2k\\) points covered by the sunny lines, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(n(n-k)\\) points.  Hence  \n   \\[\n   2k+n(n-k)\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n   which simplifies to  \n   \\[\n   k\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil . \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Put  \n   \\[\n   r:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil ,\\qquad k_{\\max}=n-r .\n   \\]\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Take the \\(r\\) horizontal lines  \n   \\[\n   L_{b}: y=b\\qquad (b=1,2,\\dots ,r).\n   \\]\n   Each \\(L_{b}\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\) points of \\(S\\); together they cover  \n   \\[\n   \\sum_{b=1}^{r}(n+1-b)=rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   points.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   The points of \\(S\\) not yet covered are those with ordinate \\(b>r\\).  \n   For every integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k_{\\max}\\) define the pair  \n   \\[\n   P_{t}=(t,\\,n+1-t),\\qquad Q_{t}=(t+1,\\,n-t).\n   \\]\n   Both points belong to \\(S\\) because \\(t+(n+1-t)=n+1\\) and \\((t+1)+(n-t)=n+1\\).  \n   The line through \\(P_{t}\\) and \\(Q_{t}\\) has equation  \n   \\[\n   y=-x+n+2+t .\n   \\]\n   Its slope is \\(-1\\) **only** when \\(t=0\\); since \\(t\\ge1\\), the line is sunny.  \n   Moreover the two points are the only points of \\(S\\) on that line (any third point would force the line to have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting Lemma\u202f2).  Thus each such line contributes exactly two new points of \\(S\\).\n\n   The \\(k_{\\max}=n-r\\) sunny lines therefore add \\(2k_{\\max}\\) points.  Adding (1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}+2(n-r)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S|,\n   \\]\n   because \\(r=\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) lines constructed cover every point of \\(S\\) and contain exactly \\(k_{\\max}=n-\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) sunny lines, i.e. the maximal number allowed by (\u2605).\n\n5. **Realising every smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the configuration above, delete any sunny line (which removes exactly two points of \\(S\\)) and replace it by a new non\u2011sunny line that passes through those two points (for instance a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with a suitable integer \\(c\\)).  The new line is non\u2011sunny, the covering condition is restored, and the number of sunny lines decreases by one.  Repeating this operation yields configurations with any integer  \n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}=n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those described in the boxed formula.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\), then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (\\(b\\ge1\\)) the admissible abscissae satisfy\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence \\(|L\\cap S|=n+1-b\\le n\\).  The vertical case\n\\(x=a\\) is analogous, giving \\(|L\\cap S|=n+1-a\\le n\\).  For a line\n\\(x+y=c\\) we have \\(c\\ge2\\) and the points satisfy \\(a\\le c-1\\), whence\n\\(|L\\cap S|=c-1\\le n\\) because \\(c\\le n+1\\).  The three lines\n\\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\) each meet \\(S\\) in exactly \\(n\\) points, showing the\nbound is sharp. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny lines)\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny (its direction is not parallel to the three\nspecial lines), then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S|\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  Suppose three\ndistinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S\\) (\\(i=1,2,3\\)) lie on \\(L\\).  Then\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nCross\u2011multiplication yields  \n\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1)(b_3-b_2)=(a_3-a_2)(b_2-b_1).\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\n(a_3-a_1)+(b_3-b_1)=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the three points are collinear with slope \\(-1\\), which would make the\nline parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the assumption that it is sunny.\nHence at most two points of \\(S\\) can lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\)\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f2 the sunny lines together\ncover at most \\(2k\\) points; by Lemma\u202f1 the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines cover at most \\(n(n-k)\\) points.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n2k+n(n-k)\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(k\\) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have more than the right\u2011hand side of (3) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining the bound\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nr:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil ,\\qquad k_{\\max}=n-r .\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le r\\) take the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_{b}: y=b .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines are distinct and non\u2011sunny.  For a fixed \\(b\\) the points\nof \\(S\\) on \\(L_{b}\\) are \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence\n\\(|L_{b}\\cap S|=n+1-b\\).  Summing over \\(b\\) we obtain the number of points\ncovered by the horizontal lines:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=1}^{r}(n+1-b)=rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n**(ii) Sunny lines.**  \nThe points of \\(S\\) not yet covered are precisely those with ordinate\n\\(b>r\\).  For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k_{\\max}\\) define the two\npoints  \n\n\\[\nP_{t}=(t,\\,n+1-t),\\qquad Q_{t}=(t+1,\\,n-t).\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\), so they belong to \\(S\\) and\nhave \\(b>r\\) because \\(t\\le k_{\\max}=n-r\\).  The line through \\(P_{t}\\) and\n\\(Q_{t}\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-x+n+2+t .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-1\\) only when \\(t=0\\); since \\(t\\ge1\\) the line is sunny.\nMoreover, any third point of \\(S\\) on this line would have to satisfy\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) and also lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which would make the\nline non\u2011sunny, contradicting Lemma\u202f2.  Hence each such line contains\nexactly the two prescribed points and no others.\n\nThus the \\(k_{\\max}=n-r\\) sunny lines contribute \\(2k_{\\max}\\) new points.\n\n**(iii) Counting.**  \nAdding (4) and the contribution of the sunny lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(rn-\\tfrac{r(r-1)}{2}\\bigr)+2(n-r)\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S|,\n\\]\n\nwhere the equality follows from the definition of \\(r\\).  Consequently the\n\\(n\\) lines constructed cover every point of \\(S\\) and contain exactly\n\\(k_{\\max}=n-\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) sunny lines, i.e. the maximal\nnumber allowed by (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Obtaining all smaller values of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from the configuration above, delete any sunny line; this removes\nexactly the two points that it uniquely covered.  Insert a new non\u2011sunny\nline (for instance a vertical line \\(x=c\\) passing through those two\npoints; such a line is never parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or\n\\(x+y=0\\) because the two points have different abscissae).  The covering\ncondition is restored, the total number of lines stays \\(n\\), and the\nnumber of sunny lines decreases by one.  Repeating this operation yields\nconfigurations with any integer  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}=n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are\nexactly the integers satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\, } .\n\\]\n\nBoth the upper bound and the realizability of every value in the interval\nhave been proved rigorously. \u220e"
