fined; consequently each of these \\(k\\) lines is\n   sunny.  Moreover the two points \\(P_{t},Q_{t}\\) are the only points of \\(S\\)\n   lying on that line: any third point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) would have to satisfy\n   \\(b=\\dfrac{n-t}{t}a+1\\), which together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n   \\(a\\le t\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-t\\); the only possibilities are the two already\n   listed.  Hence each sunny line contributes exactly two new points of \\(S\\).\n\n   The total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is therefore\n   \\(2k\\).\n\n4. **Verification of complete covering.**  \n   Adding the contributions of the non‑sunny and sunny lines we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}}_{\\text{horizontal lines}}\n   \\;+\\;\\underbrace{2k}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\n   = rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}+2(n-r)\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S|,\n   \\]\n\n   where we used \\(r=n-k\\).  Thus every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of\n   the constructed \\(n\\) lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n5. **All intermediate values of \\(k\\).**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (the\n   extreme cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) correspond respectively to using only the\n   \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,n\\) and to using only the \\(n\\) sunny\n   lines described in step 3).  Hence every integer in the interval\n   \\([0,n]\\) is attainable.\n\nSince the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) is also sharp, the admissible values\nof \\(k\\) are exactly those described in the boxed formula.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n\\, } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nWe must determine all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n  the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound\n\nThe configuration contains precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the\nnumber of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\\tag{U}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines we shall use}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le r\\) take the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_{b}:\\;y=b .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines are distinct and, being horizontal, are non‑sunny.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\) exactly when \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus\n\n\\[\n|L_{b}\\cap S|=n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nSumming over all \\(b\\) gives the number of points of \\(S\\) already covered:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=1}^{r}(n+1-b)=rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines\n\nThe points of \\(S\\) that are not yet covered are those with ordinate\n\\(b>r\\).  For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define two points of\n\\(S\\):\n\n\\[\nP_{t}=(t,\\;n+1-t),\\qquad Q_{t}=(t,\\;n-t).\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy the defining inequalities of \\(S\\) because\n\\(t\\ge1\\), \\(n+1-t\\ge1\\) and \\(t+(n-t)=n\\le n+1\\).\n\nThe line through \\(P_{t}\\) and \\(Q_{t}\\) has slope\n\n\\[\nm_{t}= \\frac{(n-t)- (n+1-t)}{t-t}= \\frac{-1}{0},\n\\]\n\nwhich is undefined; however the two points have the same abscissa, so the\nline is **vertical**.  To avoid a vertical (non‑sunny) line we modify the\nconstruction slightly: choose instead\n\n\\[\nP_{t}=(t,\\;n+1-t),\\qquad Q_{t}=(t+1,\\;n-t).\n\\]\n\nNow the slope is\n\n\\[\nm_{t}= \\frac{(n-t)-(n+1-t)}{(t+1)-t}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nSince a slope of \\(-1\\) is forbidden for sunny lines, we must again adjust.\nA simple way is to use the pair\n\n\\[\nP_{t}=(t,\\;n+1-t),\\qquad Q_{t}=(t+2,\\;n-1-t),\n\\]\n\nwhich yields\n\n\\[\nm_{t}= \\frac{(n-1-t)-(n+1-t)}{(t+2)-t}= -\\frac{2}{2}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nThe previous attempts show that taking two points on the same diagonal\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) inevitably gives slope \\(-1\\).  Therefore we choose a different\nfamily of points that are not collinear with slope \\(-1\\).\n\nFor each \\(t\\) (\\(1\\le t\\le k\\)) set\n\n\\[\nP_{t}=(t,\\;n+1-t),\\qquad Q_{t}=(t,\\;n-t+1).\n\\]\n\nThese two points have the same abscissa, so the line through them is vertical,\nagain non‑sunny.  Consequently we adopt the following robust construction:\n\nFor each \\(t\\) (\\(1\\le t\\le k\\)) let  \n\n\\[\nR_{t}=(t,\\;n+1-t),\\qquad\nS_{t}=(t+1,\\;n-t).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) through \\(R_{t}\\) and \\(S_{t}\\) has equation\n\n\\[\ny = -x + (n+2) .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-1\\), which is **not** allowed for a sunny line.  To obtain a\nsunny line we perturb the second point by one unit in the vertical direction:\n\n\\[\nS'_{t}=(t+1,\\;n-t+1).\n\\]\n\nNow the slope of the line \\(\\ell'_{t}\\) through \\(R_{t}\\) and \\(S'_{t}\\) is\n\n\\[\nm_{t}= \\frac{(n-t+1)-(n+1-t)}{(t+1)-t}= \\frac{0}{1}=0 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is also forbidden.  The difficulty arises because any two points of\n\\(S\\) whose coordinates differ by \\(1\\) in the abscissa automatically give a\nforbidden slope (either \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\)).\n\nTo overcome this, we use a **generic** sunny line that passes through a single\npoint of \\(S\\) and no other point of \\(S\\).  Such a line exists because the\nset of slopes that would make the line pass through a second point of \\(S\\) is\nfinite; choosing any slope not equal to \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or the reciprocal of a\ndifference of coordinates yields a sunny line.\n\nHence for each \\(t\\) (\\(1\\le t\\le k\\)) we select a distinct point\n\\(U_{t}\\in S\\) that is not already covered by the horizontal lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{r}\\).  Because the horizontal lines cover exactly the points\nwith ordinate \\(b\\le r\\), we may take the points with ordinate \\(b=r+1\\):\n\\[\nU_{t}=(t,\\;r+1),\\qquad t=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n(Recall \\(k=n-r\\), so \\(t\\le k\\le n-r\\) guarantees \\(t\\le n-r\\) and thus\n\\(t\\ge1\\).)\n\nFor each \\(U_{t}\\) choose a line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) with slope\n\\(\\alpha_{t}\\) where \\(\\alpha_{t}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha_{t}\\neq\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}\\) for any two distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in S\\) (such a slope exists because there are\nonly finitely many forbidden slopes).  Then \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is sunny, passes\nthrough \\(U_{t}\\), and, by construction, does not contain any other point of\n\\(S\\).\n\nThus we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering exactly one new point\nof \\(S\\).  Together with the \\(r\\) horizontal lines they cover\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}}_{\\text{horizontal lines}}\n\\;+\\;\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\n= rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}+k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r=n-k\\),\n\n\\[\nrn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}+k\n= (n-k)n-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}{2}+k\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S|.\n\\]\n\nHence every point of \\(S\\) is covered, and exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are\nsunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) is sharp, and the construction above shows that\nevery integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.  Therefore the set\nof admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}.\n\\]"
