\\[\ny = -x + (n+2) .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-1\\), which is **not** allowed for a sunny line.  To obtain a\nsunny line we perturb the second point by one unit in the vertical direction:\n\n\\[\nS'_{t}=(t+1,\\;n-t+1).\n\\]\n\nNow the slope of the line \\(\\ell'_{t}\\) through \\(R_{t}\\) and \\(S'_{t}\\) is\n\n\\[\nm_{t}= \\frac{(n-t+1)-(n+1-t)}{(t+1)-t}= \\frac{0}{1}=0 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is also forbidden.  The difficulty arises because any two points of\n\\(S\\) whose coordinates differ by \\(1\\) in the abscissa automatically give a\nforbidden slope (either \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\)).\n\nTo overcome this, we use a **generic** sunny line that passes through a single\npoint of \\(S\\) and no other point of \\(S\\).  Such a line exists because the\nset of slopes that would make the line pass through a second point of \\(S\\) is\nfinite; choosing any slope not equal to \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or the reciprocal of a\ndifference of coordinates yields a sunny line.\n\nHence for each \\(t\\) (\\(1\\le t\\le k\\)) we select a distinct point\n\\(U_{t}\\in S\\) that is not already covered by the horizontal lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{r}\\).  Because the horizontal lines cover exactly the points\nwith ordinate \\(b\\le r\\), we may take the points with ordinate \\(b=r+1\\):\n\\[\nU_{t}=(t,\\;r+1),\\qquad t=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n(Recall \\(k=n-r\\), so \\(t\\le k\\le n-r\\) guarantees \\(t\\le n-r\\) and thus\n\\(t\\ge1\\).)\n\nFor each \\(U_{t}\\) choose a line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) with slope\n\\(\\alpha_{t}\\) where \\(\\alpha_{t}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha_{t}\\neq\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}\\) for any two distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in S\\) (such a slope exists because there are\nonly finitely many forbidden slopes).  Then \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is sunny, passes\nthrough \\(U_{t}\\), and, by construction, does not contain any other point of\n\\(S\\).\n\nThus we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering exactly one new point\nof \\(S\\).  Together with t