b)\\in S\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell .\n\\tag{A}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines we shall use}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny (horizontal) lines\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{b}:\\;y=b .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines are horizontal, hence non‑sunny, and are pairwise distinct.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\) exactly when \\(b\\le r\\); for such a\nfixed \\(b\\) the admissible abscissae satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n|L_{b}\\cap S| = n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nSumming over all horizontal lines gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=1}^{r}|L_{b}\\cap S|\n   =\\sum_{b=1}^{r}(n+1-b)\n   = rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the horizontal lines cover precisely the points of \\(S\\) whose second\ncoordinate does not exceed \\(r\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines (slope \\(1\\))\n\nThe remaining points are\n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid b>r\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(b=r+q\\) with \\(q\\ge 1\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes\n\\(a\\le n+1-r-q\\).  Hence for each \\(q\\) the set of points of \\(T\\) with\nordinate \\(b=r+q\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,r+q)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r-q\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor such a point the difference \\(a-b\\) equals \\(a-(r+q)\\).  As \\(a\\) runs\nthrough the allowed interval, this difference runs through the integers\n\n\\[\n0,1,\\dots ,k-q .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le k-1\\) the set\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T\\mid a-b=c\\}\n\\]\n\nis non‑empty; in fact it consists of the points\n\n\\[\n(c+1+r,\\,r+1),\\;(c+2+r,\\,r+2),\\dots ,\\;(c+k,\\,r+k).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDefine, for each \\(c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{c}:\\; y = x - c . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is sunny.  By construction (3), \\(\\ell_{c}\\)\ncontains exactly the points of \\(T\\) whose difference \\(a-b\\) equals \\(c\\), and\ncontains no point of \\(S\\) with a different difference.  The lines\n\\(\\ell_{0},\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\) are distinct because they have different\nintercepts.\n\nFrom (3) we see that the total number of points of \\(T\\) covered by the sunny\nlines is\n\n\\[\n|T| = 1+2+\\dots +k = \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Verification that all points are covered\n\nAdding the numbers of points covered by the horizontal and sunny lines, using\n\\(r=n-k\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|S|\n&= \\Bigl(rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}\\Bigr) + \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}  \\\\\n&= (n-k)n-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}{2}+\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}   \\\\\n&= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhich indeed equals \\(|S|\\) (the cardinality of the required set).  Hence every\n\\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on at least one of the constructed \\(n\\) lines, and exactly\n\\(k\\) of those lines (the \\(\\ell_{c}\\)’s) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nThe trivial bound (1) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n\\)\nsunny lines.  The construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}.\n\\]"
