            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a family\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in S\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell \\tag{A}\n\\]\n\nand such that exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny* (i.e. their slope\nis different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines we shall use}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny (horizontal) lines\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{b}:\\;y=b .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines are horizontal, therefore non‑sunny, and are pairwise\ndistinct.  A point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\) exactly when \\(b\\le r\\); for\nsuch a fixed \\(b\\) the admissible abscissae satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|L_{b}\\cap S| = n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nSumming over all horizontal lines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=1}^{r}|L_{b}\\cap S|\n   =\\sum_{b=1}^{r}(n+1-b)\n   = rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the horizontal lines cover precisely the points of \\(S\\) whose second\ncoordinate does not exceed \\(r\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines (slope \\(1\\))\n\nThe points of \\(S\\) not yet covered are  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid b>r\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(b=r+q\\) with \\(q\\ge 1\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-r-q .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, for any \\((a,b)\\in T\\),\n\n\\[\na-b = a-(r+q)\\le n+1-r-q-(r+q)=k-q\\le -1,\n\\]\nand because \\(a\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\na-b \\ge 1-(r+q)= -\\bigl(k+q-1\\bigr)\\ge -k .\n\\]\n\nHence the possible values of the difference \\(a-b\\) for points of \\(T\\) are\nexactly the integers  \n\n\\[\n-\\,k,\\,-\\,k+1,\\dots , -1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{c}:\\; y = x + c . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is sunny.  A point \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n\\(\\ell_{c}\\) iff \\(b-a=c\\), i.e. \\(a-b=-c\\).  By (3) the set of points of \\(T\\)\nwith \\(a-b=-c\\) is non‑empty, and \\(\\ell_{c}\\) contains **exactly** those points.\nThe lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) are distinct (different intercepts) and\ntogether cover every point of \\(T\\).\n\nThe number of points of \\(T\\) covered by the sunny lines is therefore  \n\n\\[\n|T| = 1+2+\\dots +k = \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  All points are covered\n\nAdding the contributions (2) and (5) and substituting \\(r=n-k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|S|\n&= rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}+\\frac{k(k+1)}{2} \\\\\n&= (n-k)n-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}{2}+\\frac{k(k+1)}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhich equals the cardinality of \\(S\\).  Hence condition (A) is satisfied, and\nexactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines (the \\(\\ell_{c}\\)’s) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completeness of the range of \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\):\n* \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all non‑sunny).\n* \\(k=n\\): take the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) (no horizontal\n  line needed).\n* Intermediate values follow from the construction above.\n\nCombined with the trivial bound (1), no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
