            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nWe must determine all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a\nfamily \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in S\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell \\tag{A}\n\\]\n\nand such that exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny* (i.e. their\nslope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence any admissible \\(k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines we shall use}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny (horizontal) lines\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{b}:\\;y=b .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines are horizontal, therefore non‑sunny, and are pairwise\ndistinct.  A point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\) exactly when \\(b\\le r\\);\nfor such a fixed \\(b\\) the admissible abscissae satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\).\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|L_{b}\\cap S| = n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nSumming over all horizontal lines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=1}^{r}|L_{b}\\cap S|\n   =\\sum_{b=1}^{r}(n+1-b)\n   = rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the horizontal lines cover precisely the points of \\(S\\) whose\nsecond coordinate does not exceed \\(r\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines (slope \\(1\\))\n\nThe points of \\(S\\) not yet covered are  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid b>r\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(b=r+q\\) with \\(q\\ge1\\).  From the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-r-q .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nb-a = (r+q)-a \\ge (r+q)-(n+1-r-q)=2r+2q-n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(r=n-k\\),\n\n\\[\nb-a \\ge 2(n-k)+2q-n-1 = n-2k+2q-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe smallest possible value occurs for \\(q=1\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge n-2k+1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, \\(a\\ge1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\le (n+1-a)-a = n+1-2a\\le n-1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(T\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1\\;\\le\\;b-a\\;\\le\\;n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe interval \\([\\,n-2k+1,\\;n-1\\,]\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) consecutive\nintegers; denote them by  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}=n-2k+1,\\;d_{2}=n-2k+2,\\dots ,d_{k}=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\; y = x + d_{i}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is **sunny**.\nA point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{i}\\) iff \\(b-a=d_{i}\\).  By (3)–(4) the set\n\\(T\\) is exactly the disjoint union of the \\(k\\) diagonals\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid b-a=d_{i}\\}\\); consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) cover **all** points of \\(T\\).\n\nThe number of points covered by the sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n|T| = 1+2+\\dots +k = \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  All points are covered\n\nAdding the contributions (2) and (6) and substituting \\(r=n-k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{b=1}^{r}L_{b}\\bigr| + |T|\n&= rn-\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}+\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\\\\n&= (n-k)n-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}{2}+\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\\\\n&= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence condition (A) holds, and exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines (the\n\\(\\ell_{i}\\)’s) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completeness of the range of \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\):\n* \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all\n  non‑sunny).\n* \\(k=n\\): take the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) (no\n  horizontal line needed).\n* Intermediate values follow from the construction above.\n\nTogether with the bound (1), no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
