            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).  \nWe must determine all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a\nfamily \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in S\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell \\tag{A}\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) members of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny\nlines cannot exceed \\(n\\); therefore any admissible \\(k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines we shall use}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny (horizontal) lines\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{b}:\\;y=b .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines are horizontal, hence non‑sunny, and are pairwise\ndistinct.  A point \\((a,b)\\in S\\)