e.  \n     Concretely, for any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) replace the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) by the line\n     \\[\n     H_t:\\; y = t + \\varepsilon (x-t) ,\n     \\]\n     where \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a small non\u2011zero rational number chosen so that the new line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions and does not become coincident with any other line already present.  \n     The new line still contains the two lattice points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((1,t)\\); all other points formerly on \\(y=t\\) are either retained (if they also satisfy the new equation) or are covered by other lines of the original family. Because the set \\(T_n\\) is triangular, each replacement reduces the number of points that must be covered by the remaining lines by at most one.\n\n   - **Induction on the number of sunny lines.**  \n     Starting from the base configuration (all non\u2011sunny), perform the above replacement successively on distinct horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=k\\). After the \\(i\\)-th replacement we have exactly \\(i\\) sunny lines and still \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\). The induction step is justified because the points possibly lost after the \\(i\\)-th replacement are precisely the points \\((i+1,i+1)\\), which are already covered by the line \\(x+y=n+1\\). Hence the covering property is preserved.\n\n   - **Achieving any \\(k\\).**  \n     By carrying out the replacement procedure for any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n     For the remaining two values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) we modify the construction slightly: keep the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) non\u2011sunny and turn the two vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2\\) into sunny lines using the same perturbation technique; this yields \\(k=n-1\\). Finally, turn also the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) into a sunny line (its slope is \\(-1\\), so a small perturbation makes it sunny) while keeping the other \\(n-1\\) lines sunny as above; this gives \\(k=n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA line that is **not** sunny is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\nSuch a line can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\):\nif it contained two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) then the slope would be\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\in\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\ncontradicting the definition of a non\u2011sunny line.\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\nIf among the \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny, then at most \\(n-k\\) points of \\(T_n\\) are covered by the non\u2011sunny lines, and the remaining\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-k)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2+k\n\\]\npoints must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nA sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\); otherwise the three points would determine a slope belonging to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\). Consequently each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\). Therefore we must have  \n\n\\[\n2k\\ge \\frac{n(n-1)}2+k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge n .\n\\]\n\nSince obviously \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A family with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nM_1 &: x=1,\\\\\nM_2 &: x=2,\\\\\nN   &: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to one of the forbidden directions, hence none is sunny.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le 2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_a\\).  \nIf \\(a\\ge 3\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-2\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines, proving the existence for \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Turning a horizontal line into a sunny one\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\).  \nReplace the horizontal line \\(L_t: y=t\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_t:\\; y = t + \\varepsilon (x-t),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a non\u2011zero rational number chosen so small that  \n\n* \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions (its slope is \\(\\varepsilon\\neq 0,-1\\));\n* \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) does not coincide with any other line of the configuration;\n* the two lattice points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((1,t)\\) satisfy the new equation (they do because substituting \\(x=t\\) or \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=t\\) or \\(y=1\\) respectively).\n\nConsequently \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) is sunny and still contains the two points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((1,t)\\) of \\(T_n\\).  \nAll other points that were on \\(L_t\\) are of the form \\((t,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\); each such point is also on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) because \\(t+b\\le n+1\\). Hence after the replacement every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Induction producing any number of sunny lines\n\nStart from the configuration of Section\u202f2 (no sunny lines).  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) (with \\(k\\le n-2\\)) replace the horizontal line \\(L_i\\) by \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) as described above.  \nAfter the \\(i\\)-th step we have exactly \\(i\\) sunny lines, all other lines are unchanged, and the covering property still holds by the argument of Section\u202f3.  \nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we obtain a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n* **\\(k=n-1\\).**  \n  Keep the line \\(N: x+y=n+1\\) non\u2011sunny.  \n  Replace the two vertical lines \\(M_1\\) and \\(M_2\\) by the sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\widetilde M_j:\\; x = j + \\delta_j y\\qquad (j=1,2),\n  \\]\n\n  where \\(\\delta_j\\neq 0,-1\\) are small rational numbers chosen so that each \\(\\widetilde M_j\\) still passes through the lattice point \\((j,1)\\) and does not intersect any other line of the family.  \n  As before, any point of \\(T_n\\) that might be lost from a vertical line is either \\((j,1)\\) (still present) or lies on a horizontal line already present. Hence we obtain exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  Apply the perturbation of the previous paragraph to *all* three families of lines (the horizontals, the verticals, and the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n  After perturbing each of the \\(n\\) original lines we obtain \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n  The same covering argument shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) remains on at least one of the perturbed lines, because each point \\((a,b)\\) is still on the perturbed line that originally contained it (the perturbation was chosen to keep the point \\((a,b)\\) on the line).\n\nThus configurations exist for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\le n\\)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely