            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must find, for a given \\(n\\ge3\\), all \\(k\\ge0\\) for which there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, clearly \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThus only the values \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\) can possibly occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A base family with no sunny line\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_i &: y=i \\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-3),\\\\\nV_1 &: x=1,\\\\\nV_2 &: x=2,\\\\\nD   &: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDenote \\(\\mathcal F_0=\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-3}\\cup\\{V_1,V_2,D\\}\\).  \nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) is \\((n-3)+2+1=n\\).  \nEach line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so none is sunny.\n\n**Lemma 2.1 (Coverage of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\)).**  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\), we have \\(b\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le n-3\\).*  \nThen the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (which is in \\(\\mathcal F_0\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b=n-2\\).*  \nThen \\(a\\le 3\\) (since \\(a+(n-2)\\le n+1\\)).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(V_1\\); if \\(a=2\\) it lies on \\(V_2\\); if \\(a=3\\) we have \\(a+b=3+(n-2)=n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).\n\nNo other values of \\(b\\) are possible because \\(b=n-1\\) would force \\(a\\le0\\). ∎\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) satisfies the covering requirement and contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Perturbing a line to make it sunny\n\nWe now describe how to replace a line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) by a sunny line while preserving the covering property and keeping all lines distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1. Perturbing a horizontal line\n\nFix \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\).  \nConsider the line\n\n\\[\n\\widetilde H_t:\\; y = t + \\varepsilon (x-t),\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) will be chosen later.\n\n*Properties of \\(\\widetilde H_t\\).*\n\n1. **Sunny.**  \n   Its slope is \\(\\varepsilon\\).  By requiring \\(\\varepsilon\\neq0,\\,-1\\) we avoid the three forbidden directions, so \\(\\widetilde H_t\\) is sunny.\n\n2. **Passes through two lattice points of the original line.**  \n   Substituting \\(x=t\\) gives \\(y=t\\); substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=1\\).  \n   Hence \\((t,1)\\) and \\((1,t)\\) lie on \\(\\widetilde H_t\\).\n\n3. **Does not coincide with any other line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).**  \n   The only lines of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) that contain either \\((t,1)\\) or \\((1,t)\\) are \\(V_1\\) (through \\((1,1)\\)), \\(V_2\\) (through \\((2,1)\\)), and the horizontals \\(H_i\\).  \n   The slope of the line joining \\((t,1)\\) to any point of another line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) is a rational number of the form \\(\\frac{j-t}{i-t}\\) with \\((i,j)\\) taken from the finite set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t=\\{(1,1),(2,1)\\}\\cup\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Choose \\(\\varepsilon\\) rational, non‑zero, different from \\(-1\\) and different from every element of the finite set \\(\\{\\frac{j-t}{i-t}\\mid (i,j)\\in S_t\\}\\).  \n   Such a choice is possible because the rationals are infinite while the forbidden set is finite.\n\n4. **Preserves coverage.**  \n   Any point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that originally lay on the horizontal line \\(H_t\\) has the form \\((t,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(t+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   If \\(b=1\\) or \\(b=t\\) the point is still on \\(\\widetilde H_t\\) (by property 2).  \n   If \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(t+b\\le n+1\\) implies that the point also lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n   Hence after replacing \\(H_t\\) by \\(\\widetilde H_t\\) every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remains covered.\n\nThus the replacement of \\(H_t\\) by \\(\\widetilde H_t\\) adds one sunny line and leaves all required points covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2. Perturbing a vertical line\n\nFor \\(j\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) define\n\n\\[\n\\widetilde V_j:\\; x = j + \\delta_j y,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\delta_j\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) to be chosen.\n\n*Properties.*\n\n1. **Sunny.**  \n   The slope of \\(\\widetilde V_j\\) is \\(-1/\\delta_j\\); requiring \\(\\delta_j\\neq0,\\,-1\\) guarantees that the line is not vertical, horizontal, nor of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Passes through two lattice points of the original line.**  \n   Substituting \\(y=1\\) gives \\(x=j+\\delta_j\\); we choose \\(\\delta_j\\) so that \\(\\delta_j=0\\) is avoided, hence \\((j,1)\\) lies on \\(\\widetilde V_j\\).  \n   Substituting \\(y=j\\) yields \\(x=1\\); thus \\((1,j)\\) also lies on \\(\\widetilde V_j\\).\n\n3. **Avoids coincidence with other lines.**  \n   As before, the slopes that would make \\(\\widetilde V_j\\) pass through a point of another line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) form a finite set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_j=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{i-j}{k}\\;\\Big|\\;(i,k)\\in\\{(1,1),(2,1)\\}\\cup\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Choose \\(\\delta_j\\) rational, non‑zero, different from \\(-1\\) and from every element of \\(T_j\\).  This is possible because \\(T_j\\) is finite.\n\n4. **Preserves coverage.**  \n   Any point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that originally lay on the vertical line \\(V_j\\) has the form \\((a,j)\\) with \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+j\\le n+1\\).  \n   If \\(a=1\\) or \\(a=j\\) the point remains on \\(\\widetilde V_j\\).  \n   If \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\le n-3\\) then the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H_a\\); if \\(a=n-2\\) it lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n   Hence coverage is unchanged.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3. Perturbing the diagonal\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde D:\\; x+y = n+1+\\eta,\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\eta\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0\\}\\).  \nChoosing \\(\\eta\\) so that \\(\\widetilde D\\) does not pass through any lattice point already on another line of the family is again possible because the forbidden values of \\(\\eta\\) form a finite set.  \nThe slope of \\(\\widetilde D\\) is \\(-1\\), but the line is *not* parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) because of the non‑zero intercept shift; nevertheless, the definition of “sunny’’ concerns parallelism, not coincidence, so \\(\\widetilde D\\) is sunny (its direction is the same as the forbidden line, but the line itself is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to a line of slope \\(-1\\) that passes through the origin; the problem’s wording “parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)” means having the same slope, i.e. slope \\(-1\\). Hence \\(\\widetilde D\\) **is not sunny**.  \nTo obtain a sunny diagonal we instead replace the diagonal by a line of a different slope, e.g.\n\n\\[\n\\widetilde D':\\; y = \\lambda x + \\mu,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\mu\\) chosen so that all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) remain covered (they are already covered by the horizontals or verticals).  For the purpose of the construction we never need to make the diagonal sunny; we can achieve any required number \\(k\\) of sunny lines by perturbing the horizontals and/or the two verticals only.  Consequently the diagonal may stay non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Select* any subset \\(\\mathcal S\\) of \\(k\\) distinct lines from \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).  \nFor each line in \\(\\mathcal S\\) perform the appropriate perturbation described in Sections 3.1 or 3.2 (horizontal or vertical).  \nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Verification.*\n\n* Number of lines: each perturbation replaces a line by another line, so \\(|\\mathcal F_k|=n\\).\n* Distinctness: the choices of \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta_j\\) avoid all slopes that would cause coincidence with any other line, hence all lines in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) are distinct.\n* Sunny count: exactly the lines that were perturbed become sunny; the unperturbed lines remain non‑sunny.  Thus \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n* Coverage: Lemma 2.1 guarantees coverage for the unperturbed lines; the arguments of Sections 3.1 and 3.2 show that each perturbation does not destroy coverage.  Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nTherefore for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nCombining the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) with the explicit constructions for all admissible \\(k\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non‑negative integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
