ines \\(x=t\\) (\\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\)) by the corresponding sunny lines \\(\\ell_t\\).  \n   Each replacement adds exactly one sunny line and, by Lemma\u202f2, does not destroy the covering property. The line \\(x=n-1\\) cannot be turned sunny (any line through \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) is vertical), so at most the \\(n-2\\) columns with indices \\(1,\\dots ,n-2\\) can become sunny.\n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). Choose any \\(k\\) distinct indices \\(t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\) from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and replace the vertical lines \\(x=t_i\\) by \\(\\ell_{t_i}\\).  \n   The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 every admissible family must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines; therefore the number of sunny lines can never reach \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\).\n\nCombining the upper bound (1) with the explicit constructions yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non\u2011negative integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal L\\) a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nAt least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non\u2011sunny; consequently \\(