"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 trivially \\(k\\le n\\) because there are only \\(n\\) lines.  \n2. **Construction for any \\(k\\in[0,n]\\).**  \n   *Cover the outer diagonal* \\(a+b=n+1\\) (the points \\((1,n),\\dots ,(n,1)\\)) with three non\u2011sunny lines: a horizontal line \\(y=n\\), a vertical line \\(x=n\\) and the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   *Add sunny lines* one by one. Each time we pick a still\u2011uncovered lattice point \\((a,b)\\) (such a point exists while the covering is incomplete) and draw through it a line whose slope is chosen to be different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of all previously drawn lines. This line is sunny, distinct from the existing ones, and covers the chosen point. Repeating this step we can create as many sunny lines as we wish, up to the prescribed number \\(k\\).  \n   *Adjust the total number of lines to exactly \\(n\\).*  \n   \u2013 If after adding the required \\(k\\) sunny lines we have fewer than \\(n\\) lines, we append extra non\u2011sunny lines (parallel to one of the three forbidden directions) that pass through already\u2011covered points; they do not affect the covering property.  \n   \u2013 If we have more than \\(n\\) lines, we simply discard any surplus non\u2011sunny lines, because the covering condition is already satisfied.  \n   The procedure therefore yields a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\nThe lower bound \\(k\\ge0\\) is trivial, and the construction shows that every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), not \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and not \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nThe family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, hence at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny.  \nThus any admissible \\(k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Covering the outer diagonal\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are covered by the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=n,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=n,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) equals \\(\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\\).  \n\\(L_h\\) contains \\((1,n)\\), \\(L_v\\) contains \\((n,1)\\), and every remaining point satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\), hence lies on \\(L_d\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Adding sunny lines while preserving distinctness\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a finite set of distinct lines.  \nIf a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) does not belong to any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) whose slope is \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), then there exists a sunny line through \\(P\\) that is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set of slopes of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is finite. Choose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope of }L\\mid L\\in\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because we are removing only finitely many numbers from \\(\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\).  \nThe line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\alpha\\) is sunny (its slope is none of the forbidden three) and, by construction, cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\in[0,n]\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and a target integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Initial covering.**  \n   Start with the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\) from Lemma\u202f1.  \n   Denote the current family by \\(\\mathcal L_0=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\).  \n   At this stage all points of the outer diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) are covered; the interior points of \\(T_n\\) (those with \\(a+b\\le n\\)) may remain uncovered.\n\n2. **Insertion of sunny lines.**  \n   While the number of sunny lines in the current family is smaller than \\(k\\) do:\n   * Choose a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that is not yet covered by any sunny line of the family. Such a point exists as long as we have added fewer than \\(k\\) sunny lines, because each iteration will cover at least one new point.\n   * Apply Lemma\u202f2 to obtain a sunny line \\(S\\) through \\(P\\) that is distinct from all lines already present.\n   * Add \\(S\\) to the family.\n\n   Each iteration increases the total number of lines by one and the number of sunny lines by one, while covering at least one previously uncovered point. Because \\(|T_n|<\\infty\\), the process terminates after at most \\(|T_n|\\) steps, and we stop exactly when we have introduced \\(k\\) sunny lines. Let the resulting family be \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n3. **Ensuring exactly \\(n\\) lines.**  \n   *If \\(|\\mathcal L|<n\\):*  \n   Append \\(n-|\\mathcal L|\\) additional non\u2011sunny lines. For each needed line we may take a copy of \\(L_h\\) shifted vertically by an integer amount that does not coincide with any existing line (e.g. \\(y=n+1, n+2,\\dots\\)). These extra lines pass through points already covered, so the covering condition remains satisfied.  \n   *If \\(|\\mathcal L|>n\\):*  \n   Since the covering condition is already fulfilled after the previous steps, we may delete any surplus non\u2011sunny lines (they are not needed). Deleting a line cannot uncover a point that was covered by a sunny line, and every interior point was covered by a sunny line by construction. Hence we can reduce the family to exactly \\(n\\) lines without affecting the covering property.\n\n   After this adjustment we have a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Existence for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nFor \\(k=0\\) we simply keep the three non\u2011sunny lines of Lemma\u202f1 and add \\(n-3\\) further non\u2011sunny lines; all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered because the three lines already cover the whole set (the interior points lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \nFor \\(k=n\\) we repeatedly apply Lemma\u202f2 until we have introduced \\(n\\) sunny lines; no non\u2011sunny line is then needed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is also sufficient: for each such \\(k\\) a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members satisfying the required covering property exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All admissible values are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
