n\nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are taken from the three forbidden families:\n* one vertical line \\(x=c\\),\n* one horizontal line \\(y=c\\),\n* one line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\),\n\nwith the constants \\(c\\) chosen so that the lines are distinct and together they cover all points of \\(T_n\\) not already covered by the sunny lines.  A concrete choice is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{k+1}&:x=1,\\\\\nL_{k+2}&:y=1,\\\\\nL_{k+3}&:x+y=2,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nand, if more than three non\u2011sunny lines are needed, we continue adding vertical lines \\(x=2,3,\\dots\\) (or horizontal lines, or lines of slope \\(-1\\)) until we have exactly \\(n-k\\) of them.  Because each vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains all points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n+1-c\\), each horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains all points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(a\\le n+1-c\\), and each line \\(x+y=c\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\), the union of these \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines covers the whole triangular set \\(T_n\\) once the appropriate constants are taken.  A short verification shows that for any \\(k\\) the described family of \\(n\\) lines indeed covers every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 No other values of \\(k\\) are possible.*  \nIf a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny simply because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.  Moreover, a line that is not sunny must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; such a line can be chosen arbitrarily, so there is no obstruction to having zero sunny lines.  Hence the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), inclusive.\n\nThese three steps together give a complete, rigorous solution of the problem.  \n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a single line).\n\nA line that is **not** sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nA sunny line also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a line \\(L\\).  If \\(L\\) is vertical, say \\(x=c\\), then the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\) are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points because \\(c\\ge1\\).  The same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  If \\(L\\) has slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\), then the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\) are those with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\); again there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  Finally, if \\(L\\) has any other slope, the intersection of \\(L\\) with the integer lattice is a set of points whose coordinates differ by at most one when moving from one lattice point to the next along the line; because the triangle \\(T_n\\) has side length \\(n\\), at most \\(n\\) lattice points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on \\(L\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 2 (At least \\(n\\) lines are necessary).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 and (1),\n\n\\[\nn\\cdot n \\ge |T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nso at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required to cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) lines, as required in the problem, is optimal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Construction for any prescribed \\(k\\), \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k