ecause the requirement is \u201cat least one line\u201d.\n\nThus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (No other values of \\(k\\) can occur).\n\nIf a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, the number of sunny lines among them is a non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThere are only \\(n\\) lines in the family, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  The definition of \u201csunny\u201d does not forbid a line from being non\u2011sunny, hence the number of sunny lines can be zero.  Consequently the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized, and Lemma\u202f4 shows that no other integer can arise.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
