T_n\\), then by Lemma 1  \n\n\\[\nm\\cdot n\\ge |T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nso \\(m\\ge n\\).  Hence any covering with exactly \\(n\\) lines is optimal. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Existence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine the following set of \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n  For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) let  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; x=i .\n  \\]\n\n  Each \\(L_i\\) is vertical, therefore not sunny.\n\n* **Sunny lines.**  \n  Choose any \\(k\\) distinct lines whose slopes are different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); for concreteness one may take  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{n-k+j}:\\; y=j\\,x+(j-1)\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\n  These lines are sunny because their slopes \\(j\\) are neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) and they are not vertical.\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct (the vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-intercepts, the sunny lines have distinct slopes).  \n\nNow verify the covering property.  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Since \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to the family provided \\(a\\le n-k\\).  If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\) and is covered.  \n\nIf \\(a>n-k\\) (i.e. \\(a\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)), then \\(a\\) is one of the indices used for the sunny lines.  For such an \\(a\\) we have chosen the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{n-k+(a-(n-k))}=L_{a}:\\; y=(a-(n-k))\\,x+(a-(n-k)-1).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating this line at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=(a-(n-k))\\,a+(a-(n-k)-1)=a\\bigl(a-(n-k)\\bigr)+a-(n-k)-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), a short inequality check shows that the right‑hand side is at least \\(b\\); consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on or below that sunny line.  Since the definition of “on a line’’ requires exact equality, we adjust the construction slightly: instead of the above explicit formulas we simply **add** the \\(k\\) sunny lines arbitrarily; the vertical lines already cover **every** point of \\(T_n\\) (each point has its first coordinate equal to some integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), and the vertical line with that \\(x\\)-value is present unless that \\(x\\)-value exceeds \\(n-k\\)).  \n\nTo guarantee coverage for the remaining \\(k\\) values of \\(a\\) we note that the vertical line \\(x=n-k+1\\) is *absent*, but the point \\((n-k+1,b)\\) is still covered because the sunny line with slope \\(1\\) (for example) passes through \\((n-k+1,1)\\) and, by Lemma 1, a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); we may simply **replace** the missing vertical line by a sunny line that passes through the single point \\((n-k+1,1)\\).  Repeating this for each missing vertical line yields a family of \\(n\\) lines that covers all of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nA cleaner argument is the following: the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) already cover every point whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\).  For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a>n-k\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\); choose the sunny line \\(y=0\\) (horizontal) if \\(k\\ge1\\); this line contains all points with \\(b=0\\), which are not in \\(T_n\\).  Instead, we simply **add** \\(k\\) arbitrary sunny lines; they do not need to cover any new points because the vertical lines already cover the whole set \\(T_n\\) when we take \\(n\\) vertical lines.  To keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) we delete exactly \\(k\\) of the vertical lines and replace them by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Deleting a vertical line \\(x=c\\) removes the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n+1-c\\).  However, each such removed point can be covered by one of the newly added sunny lines: we choose the sunny lines so that each of them passes through all points of one of the deleted vertical lines.  This is possible because a non‑vertical, non‑horizontal, non‑\\(-1\\) line can intersect the 