----\n### 3. Covering the interior points\n\nThe *interior* of \\(T_n\\) consists of the points with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nWe treat two cases.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1. The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_a\\) is non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is an interior point, then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\{V_a\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers **all** points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is \\(3+(n-1)=n+2\\).  \nWe delete any two of the vertical lines (for instance \\(V_{n-2},V_{n-1}\\)); the remaining \\(n\\) lines still cover every point because each interior point uses the vertical line with its own \\(x\\)-coordinate, and the deleted vertical lines are not needed for any point whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is different.  \nThus for \\(k=0\\) we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2. The case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nWe start with the three non‑sunny lines \\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\).  \nWe shall add exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each passing through a distinct interior point, and then adjust the total number of lines to \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2 (existence of a new sunny line).**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\) be a finite set of lines already constructed.  \nLet \\(P\\in T_n\\) be a point that does **not** lie on any line of \\(\\mathcal S\\) whose slope is \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nThen there exists a sunny line through \\(P\\) that is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n*Proof.* The set of slopes of the lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\) is finite; denote it by \\(M\\subset\\mathbb R\\cup\