fty,-1\\}\\cup M .\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because we are removing only finitely many numbers from the uncountable set \\(\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\).  \nThe line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\alpha\\) is sunny (its slope is none of the forbidden three) and, by construction, cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal S\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Construction of the sunny lines.**  \n\nInitialize \\(\\mathcal S_0=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\).  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) perform the following step:\n\n* Choose any interior point \\(P_i\\in T_n\\) that is not already on a sunny line of \\(\\mathcal S_{i-1}\\).  \n  (Such a point exists because at most \\(i-1\\) interior points have been covered by sunny lines, while the set of interior points is non‑empty for \\(n\\ge3\\).)\n\n* Apply Lemma 2 to \\(\\mathcal S_{i-1}\\) and \\(P_i\\) to obtain a sunny line \\(S_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) that is distinct from all lines already in \\(\\mathcal S_{i-1}\\).\n\n* Set \\(\\mathcal S_i=\\mathcal S_{i-1}\\cup\\{S_i\\}\\).\n\nAfter the \\(k\\)-th iteration we have  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_k=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d,S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers every interior point (each interior point lies on the sunny line that was created for it) as well as all outer‑diagonal points (by Lemma 1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Adjusting the total number of lines to \\(n\\)\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) has \\(3+k\\) lines.\n\n*If \\(3+k=n\\)*, we are done.\n\n*If \\(3+k<n\\)*, add \\(n-(3+k)\\) further non‑sunny lines.  \nFor instance, for each needed extra line choose a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with an integer \\(c\\) that does not coincide with any previously used vertical line (such integers exist because there are infinitely many).  \nThese added lines do not affect the covering property, because every point of \\(T_n\\) is already 