"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and replaced the faulty construction by a rigorous one.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) I exhibit a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contain precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (i.e. lines whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Three non\u2011sunny \u201cborder\u201d lines**  \n   \\[\n   L_h:\\;y=n,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=n,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that every point with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n2. **A family \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non\u2011sunny lines that covers the whole interior**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal N=\n   \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\}\\cup\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n   \\]\n   Lemma\u202f2 proves that every interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n3. **Adding exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines**  \n   Starting from the three border lines and the set \\(\\mathcal N\\) we replace the first \\(k\\) vertical lines\n   \\(V_i:x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) by sunny lines \\(S_i\\) constructed inductively.\n   Lemma\u202f3 guarantees that, for each \\(i\\), there exists a sunny line through the point \\((i,1)\\) whose slope is new (different from all slopes already used).  \n   The resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n+2\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n4. **Deleting two redundant non\u2011sunny lines**  \n   The two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) are removed.  \n   Every point that was covered only by one of those horizontals is now covered by the sunny line \\(S_i\\) that passes through the same column \\(x=i\\); consequently the covering property is preserved.\n\n5. **Verification**  \n   Lemma\u202f4 checks, case by case, that after the deletion each point of the required set lies on at least one remaining line.  \n   The final collection \\(\\mathcal G_k\\) therefore consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\nSince the obvious bound \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) also holds, the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the family \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (border lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=n,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=n,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll three lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) belongs to at least one of them.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n\\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(L_h\\); \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L_v\\); all the remaining points satisfy \\(x+y=n+1\\) and therefore lie on \\(L_d\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (interior covering by non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\n\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} \\;\\cup\\;\n\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) are non\u2011sunny, they are distinct and  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|=(n-3)+2=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) (the *interior* of \\(T_n\\)) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) be such a point.\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-3\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal N\\) contains the point.\n\n* If \\(a=n-2\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n\\); hence the point lies on \\(y=1\\) or \\(y=2\\).\n\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\), so the point lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\nNo other values of \\(a\\) are possible. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (existence of a new sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\) be a finite family of lines already constructed and let\n\\(P\\in T_n\\) be a point that does **not** belong to any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal S\\).\nThen there exists a sunny line through \\(P\\) whose slope is different from the slopes of all lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set of slopes of the lines of \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a finite subset \\(M\\subset\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\).\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup M .\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because we remove only finitely many numbers from the uncountable set \\(\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\).\nThe line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\alpha\\) is sunny and, by construction, has a slope not appearing in \\(\\mathcal S\\); consequently it is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal S\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\).  \nStart with the three border lines \\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\) and the family \\(\\mathcal N\\) of Lemma\u202f2.\nWrite the vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) as  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n-3).\n\\]\n\nWe shall replace the first \\(k\\) of them (\\(V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\)) by sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\nThe replacement is performed inductively.\n\n*Induction basis (\\(i=1\\)).*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal S_0=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\bigl(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{V_1\\}\\bigr)\\).  \nThe point \\(P_1=(1,1)\\) belongs to \\(V_1\\) but to no sunny line of \\(\\mathcal S_0\\).  \nApply Lemma\u202f3 with \\(P_1\\) to obtain a sunny line \\(S_1\\) through \\((1,1)\\) whose slope is new.\n\n*Induction step.*  \nAssume sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_{i-1}\\) have been constructed and let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{i-1}= \\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\n\\bigl(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{i-1}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\n\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_{i-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(P_i=(i,1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_i\\) but on no sunny line of \\(\\mathcal S_{i-1}\\) (the only sunny lines constructed so far pass through columns \\(1,\\dots ,i-1\\)).  \nLemma\u202f3 yields a sunny line \\(S_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) with a slope not occurring in \\(\\mathcal S_{i-1}\\).\n\nProceeding up to \\(i=k\\) we obtain sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\n\\bigl(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\n\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of  \n\n\\[\n3+(n-1)-k+k = n+2\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (deleting two horizontals preserves the covering)\n\nRemove the two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) from \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) and set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_k = \\mathcal F_k\\setminus\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal G_k\\) still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, Lemma\u202f1 guarantees that the point lies on \\(L_h\\), \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_d\\), all of which remain in \\(\\mathcal G_k\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b\\le n\\)* (interior points), we distinguish cases.\n\n1. **\\(a\\le n-3\\) and \\(a\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).**  \n   The vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present (it belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\) and was not removed), so the point is covered.\n\n2. **\\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).**  \n   By construction, the sunny line \\(S_a\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\).  \n   Its slope is not vertical; therefore the line \\(S_a\\) contains the whole column \\(x=a\\).  \n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_a\\) for every admissible \\(b\\).\n\n3. **\\(a=n-2\\).**  \n   Then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n   If \\(b=1\\) or \\(b=2\\) the point was on a deleted horizontal, but it also lies on the sunny line \\(S_{n-2}\\) (which passes through \\((n-2,1)\\) and, having non\u2011vertical slope, contains the whole column \\(x=n-2\\)).  \n   Hence the point is still covered.\n\n4. **\\(a=n-1\\).**  \n   Here \\(b=1\\). The point \\((n-1,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{n-1}\\) (constructed through that point), so it is covered.\n\nAll possibilities are covered, proving the claim. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the construction\n\nAfter the deletion we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal G_k| = (n+2)-2 = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)).  \nBy Lemma\u202f4 every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal G_k\\).  \n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final remark on the necessary condition\n\nSince we are asked for exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative; hence \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is necessary.  \nThe construction above shows that this condition is also sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]"
