\\le n\\) (the interior of \\(T_n\\)) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) be such a point.\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-3\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal N\\) contains the point.\n\n* If \\(a=n-2\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n\\); hence the point lies on \\(y=1\\) or \\(y=2\\).\n\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\), so the point lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\nNo other values of \\(a\\) are possible. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction of a family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;(x,y)=(i,1)+t\\,(1,\\;n-i)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\); its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{n-i-1}{1}=n-i-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(n-i-1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) for all admissible \\(i\\), each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For distinct \\(i\\) the slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct, hence the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct.\n\n2. **The full family before deletion.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\n   \\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\n   \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\) consists of the vertical lines\n   \\(x=i\\) for \\(k+1\\le i\\le n-3\\) together with the two horizontals \\(y=1,y=2\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal F_k|=3+(n-1)-k+k=n+2,\n   \\]\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of its members are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (covering after deleting the two horizontals)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_k=\\mathcal F_k\\setminus\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal G_k\\) still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1 – \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \nBy Lemma 1 the point lies on \\(L_h\\), \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_d\\); all three belong to \\(\\mathcal G_k\\).\n\n*Case 2 – \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \nWe distinguish sub‑cases.\n\n*Subcase 2.1 – \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).*  \nThe point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) by construction.  \nSince \\(S_a\\) is not vertical, it meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) only at \\((a,1)\\); however every point of the column \\(x=a\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\) can be written as a convex combination of \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,n-a)\\).  \nIndeed, for any \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-a\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a,1)+\\frac{b-1}{\\,n-a-1\\,}\\,(1,\\;n-a-1),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the same line \\(S_a\\).  \nThus all points with this \\(a\\) are covered by \\(S_a\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.2 – \\(k+1\\le a\\le n-3\\).*  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal G_k\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Subcase 2.3 – \\(a=n-2\\).*  \nThen \\(b\\le 2\\).  \nIf \\(b=1\\) the point is \\((n-2,1)\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(S_{n-2}\\) (the line through \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\)).  \nIf \\(b=2\\) we write  \n\n\\[\n(n-2,2)=(n-2,1)+\\frac{1}{\\,n-(n-2)-1\\,}\\,(1,\\;n-(n-2)-1)\n      =(n-2,1)+\\frac{1}{1}\\,(1,1),\n\\]\n\nso \\((n-2,2)\\) also belongs to \\(S_{n-2}\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.4 – \\(a=n-1\\).*  \nHere \\(b=1\\). The point \\((n-1,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{n-1}\\) (through \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,0)\\)), hence is covered.\n\nAll possibilities are covered, proving the claim. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the construction\n\nAfter the deletion we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal G_k|=(n+2)-2=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)).  \nBy Lemma 3 every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal G_k\\).  \n\nThus for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable set of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of the bound\n\nA set of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, and the number of sunny lines cannot be negative; therefore \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is necessary.  \n\nSince the construction above shows that every integer in this interval is attainable, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]"
