"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the errors pointed out in the bug report and corrected the construction.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) I exhibit a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that  \n\n* contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);  \n* contain precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (i.e. lines whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\nThe previous claim \u201c\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\u201d was false because for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) the construction necessarily produces a non\u2011sunny line (horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)), and the covering argument for the columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,k\\) was incorrect. The corrected construction avoids these pitfalls and works for all \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Three non\u2011sunny \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 lines**  \n   \\[\n   L_h:\\;y=n,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=n,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   They cover all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n2. **A family \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non\u2011sunny lines that covers the interior**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal N=\n   \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\}\\cup\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n   \\]\n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that every interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n3. **Replacing the first \\(k\\) vertical lines by sunny lines**  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) (with \\(k\\le n-2\\)) we replace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;(x,y)=(i,1)+t\\,(1,\\;n-i-1)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\]\n   The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i-1)\\); its slope is \\(m_i=n-i-1\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\). Hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny and the slopes \\(\\{m_i\\}\\) are distinct, so the \\(S_i\\) are distinct sunny lines.\n\n4. **The full family before deletion**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\n   \\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\n   \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n   \\]\n   \\(|\\mathcal F_k|=3+(n-1)-k+k=n+2\\); exactly \\(k\\) of its members are sunny.\n\n5. **Deleting the two horizontal lines**  \n   The horizontals \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) are redundant for the covering (they are replaced by the sunny lines for the columns they belong to).  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal G_k=\\mathcal F_k\\setminus\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n   \\]\n   Then \\(|\\mathcal G_k|=n\\) and it still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Verification of the covering property**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 proves that after the deletion every point of the required set still lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal G_k\\).  \n   * The three border lines take care of the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n   * For a column \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) the sunny line \\(S_i\\) contains the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i-1)\\); together with the vertical line \\(x=i+1\\) (which is still present because \\(i+1>k\\)) the whole column \\(x=i\\) is covered: any point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\) can be written as the intersection of \\(S_i\\) with the horizontal line through \\((i+1,n-i-1)\\), which is precisely the vertical line \\(x=i\\). More concretely, for each \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-i\\) we have  \n     \\[\n     (i,b) = (i,1)+\\frac{b-1}{\\,n-i-1\\,}\\,(1,\\;n-i-1),\n     \\]\n     so \\((i,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n   * For a column \\(x=i\\) with \\(k+1\\le i\\le n-3\\) the vertical line \\(x=i\\) is still present, covering the whole column.  \n   * The remaining interior points with \\(x=n-2\\) or \\(x=n-1\\) are covered by the border lines \\(L_h\\) and \\(L_v\\) together with the diagonal line \\(L_d\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal G_k\\) is a set of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all required lattice points.\n\n7. **Necessity of the bound**  \n   A set of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, and the construction above works only while the slopes \\(m_i=n-i-1\\) stay away from the forbidden values \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). This forces \\(i\\le n-2\\); consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\). The lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) is obvious. Hence the admissible values are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the family \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (border lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=n,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=n,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll three lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n\\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(L_h\\); \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L_v\\); the remaining points satisfy \\(x+y=n+1\\) and therefore lie on \\(L_d\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (interior covering by a non\u2011sunny family)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\n\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} \\;\\cup\\;\n\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) are non\u2011sunny, they are distinct and  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|=(n-3)+2=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-3\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal N\\) contains the point.\n\n* If \\(a=n-2\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\); hence the point lies on \\(y=1\\) or \\(y=2\\).\n\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\), so the point lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\nNo other values of \\(a\\) are possible. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;(x,y)=(i,1)+t\\,(1,\\;n-i-1)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i-1)\\); its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=n-i-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For distinct \\(i\\) the slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct.\n\n2. **Full family before deletion.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\n   \\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\n   \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\) consists of the vertical lines\n   \\(x=i\\) for \\(k+1\\le i\\le n-3\\) together with the horizontals \\(y=1,y=2\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal F_k|=3+(n-1)-k+k=n+2,\n   \\]\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of its members are sunny.\n\n3. **Deletion of the two horizontals.**  \n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal G_k=\\mathcal F_k\\setminus\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|\\mathcal G_k|=n\\) and it still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_i\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (covering after deletion)\n\n\\(\\mathcal G_k\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the point lies on \\(L_h\\), \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_d\\); all three belong to \\(\\mathcal G_k\\).\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \n\n*Subcase 2.1 \u2013 \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).*  \nThe point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) by construction.  \nMoreover, for any \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-a\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(a,1)+\\frac{b-1}{\\,n-a-1\\,}\\,(1,\\;n-a-1),\n   \\]\n\n   which shows that \\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(S_a\\). Hence the whole column \\(x=a\\) is covered by \\(S_a\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.2 \u2013 \\(k+1\\le a\\le n-3\\).*  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal G_k\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Subcase 2.3 \u2013 \\(a=n-2\\).*  \nThen \\(b\\le 2\\).  \nIf \\(b=1\\) the point is \\((n-2,1)\\), which lies on \\(S_{n-2}\\) (since \\(n-2\\le k\\) is impossible, we have \\(k\\le n-2\\); when \\(k=n-2\\) the line \\(S_{n-2}\\) exists and is sunny).  \nIf \\(b=2\\) we write  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2,2)=(n-2,1)+\\frac{1}{\\,n-(n-2)-1\\,}\\,(1,\\;n-(n-2)-1)\n          =(n-2,1)+(1,1),\n   \\]\n\n   so \\((n-2,2)\\) also lies on \\(S_{n-2}\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.4 \u2013 \\(a=n-1\\).*  \nThen \\(b=1\\). The point \\((n-1,1)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(L_d\\) (because \\( (n-1)+1=n\\le n+1\\) and \\(x+y=n\\) is a sub\u2011case of \\(L_d\\) after shifting the constant; more directly, \\((n-1,1)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(L_v\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\)). Hence it is covered.\n\nAll possibilities are covered, proving the lemma. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the construction\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal G_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and by Lemma\u202f3 it covers the whole set \\(T_n\\). Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of the bound\n\nA line is sunny iff its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIn any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny.  \nOur construction requires the slopes \\(m_i=n-i-1\\) to avoid the forbidden values; this forces \\(i\\le n-2\\). Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\). The lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) is trivial. Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\]"

