ree forms  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}(c): y=c,\\qquad \\ell_{y}(c): x=c,\\qquad \\ell_{d}(c): x+y=c,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwith a real constant \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  No configuration with exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S\\) and contains\nexactly one sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  The other \\(n-1\\) lines belong to the three\nfamilies (1).  Let  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{c\\mid \\ell_{x}(c)\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\;\nB=\\{c\\mid \\ell_{y}(c)\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\;\nC=\\{c\\mid \\ell_{d}(c)\\in\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are distinct, \\(|A|+|B|+|C|=n-1\\).  \nIf two of the three sets were empty, say \\(B=C=\\varnothing\\), then all non\u2011sunny\nlines are horizontal.  Any horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(S\\) only at points with\n\\(b=c\\).  Since the values of \\(b\\) occurring in \\(S\\) are the integers\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\), we would need \\(|A|\\ge n\\), contradicting \\(|A|=n-1\\).  \nThus at least two of the families (1) appear among the non\u2011sunny lines; without loss\nof generality assume \\(|A|\\ge1\\) and \\(|B|\\ge1\\).\n\nChoose a value \\(b_{0}\\notin A\\) (possible because \\(|A|\\le n-2\\)) and a value\n\\(a_{0}\\notin B\\) (possible because \\(|B|\\le n-2\\)).  The point\n\\((a_{0},b_{0})\\) belongs to \\(S\\) because we can take \\(a_{0}=b_{0}=1\\) and then\nincrease both while keeping \\(a_{0}+b_{0}\\le n+1\\); the precise choice is irrelevant,\nonly that such a point exists.  This point is not on any horizontal line\n(\\(b_{0}\\notin A\\)) nor on any vertical line (\\(a_{0}\\notin B\\)).  It is also not on the\nunique sunny line \\(\\ell\\), because \\(\\ell\\) has a slope different from \\(0\\) and\n\\(\\infty\\).  Hence \\((a_{0},b_{0})\\) is uncovered, a contradiction.  Therefore a\nconfiguration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existe