onal \\(\\ell_s\\) for \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such diagonals, so at least \\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are forced to be non‑sunny. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-(n-1)=1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince a sunny line is defined only by a property of its slope, the trivial lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) also holds. Thus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le 1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for the two possible values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) given by (1) for \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\);\n* the vertical line \\(v:\\;x=1\\).\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); none is sunny, so \\(k=0\\).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for some \\(s\\) in the range \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\); hence it lies on the diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\). Thus the covering condition is fulfilled.\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the same \\(n-1\\) diagonals as above and replace the vertical line by a sunny line, for example  \n\n\\[\ns:\\; y=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(s\\) is sunny.  \nThe diagonals still cover every point of \\(T_n\\) for the same reason as in case (i). Hence the configuration satisfies the covering requirement and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.  \nBy Lemma 1 the configuration must contain all \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nSince the total number of lines is \\(n\\), at most one line remains free to be chosen arbitrarily. Therefore at most one line can be sunny, contradicting \\(k\\ge 2\\). Hence no such configuration exists.\n\n-----------------------