            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad \n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_s\\) has slope \\(-1\\); consequently it is **not** sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(\\mathcal L\\neq\\ell_s\\) then \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most one point of each set \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAny two distinct points of a diagonal have slope \\(-1\\). If a line different from \\(\\ell_s\\) contained two such points, that line would also have slope \\(-1\\) and would therefore coincide with \\(\\ell_s\\), a contradiction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Forced diagonals\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\ell_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSuppose that a non‑empty set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1,\\;\\ell_s\\notin\\mathcal F\\}\n\\]\n\nof diagonals is omitted.\n\nFor each \\(s\\in S\\) the set \\(D_s\\) contains \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) points.  \nBy Lemma 1, any line that is not \\(\\ell_s\\) can cover at most one of those points; therefore covering all points of \\(D_s\\) requires at least \\(s-1\\) distinct lines that are not \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\nLet \\(s_{\\max}=\\max S\\).  \nThe \\(s_{\\max}-1\\) lines needed for the largest omitted diagonal each intersect any other omitted diagonal in at most one point (Lemma 1). Consequently, after those \\(s_{\\max}-1\\) lines are used, each of the remaining \\(|S|-1\\) omitted diagonals still has at least one uncovered point, and a new line is required for each of them. Hence the total number of lines that are **not** members of \\(\\mathcal D\\) is at least  \n\n\\[\n(s_{\\max}-1)+(|S|-1)=\\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)+|S|-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which \\(|\\mathcal D|-|S|=(n-1)-|S|\\) are the diagonals that are present. Thus the number of lines that are not diagonals is at most  \n\n\\[\nn-[(n-1)-|S|]=|S|+1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)+|S|-1\\le |S|+1\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nSince every omitted diagonal satisfies \\(s\\ge 3\\), we have \\(s-1\\ge 2\\).  \nTherefore \\(\\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)=2\\), which forces \\(S=\\{3\\}\\) and \\(|S|=1\\).\n\nIf \\(\\ell_{3}\\) were omitted, the two points of \\(D_{3}\\) would each need a distinct non‑diagonal line (Lemma 1). Those two lines already realise the bound \\(|S|+1=2\\) from (2), leaving no line to cover the point \\((1,1)\\in D_{2}\\) (which lies on no diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(s\\ge 3\\)). This contradiction shows that \\(\\ell_{3}\\) cannot be omitted.\n\nHence **every** diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F\\supset\\{\\ell_{3},\\ell_{4},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\na collection of \\(n-1\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for sunny lines\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, and (3) already supplies \\(n-1\\) of them, all non‑sunny. Hence at most one of the remaining lines can be sunny:\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the forced diagonals (3) and add the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  The vertical line is not sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) let \\(s=a+b\\).  \nIf \\(s\\ge 3\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\subset\\ell_s\\); if \\(s=2\\) then \\((a,b)=(1,1)\\) lies on \\(x=1\\).  Thus every required point is covered and no line is sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nReplace the vertical line by a sunny line, e.g. \\(y=2x-1\\) (slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Exactly one line of the family is sunny.  The covering argument is unchanged: points with \\(s\\ge 3\\) lie on the corresponding diagonal, and the point \\((1,1)\\) (the only point with \\(s=2\\)) lies on \\(y=2x-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nInequality (4) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\); the explicit constructions exhibit the two admissible values.  Therefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
