nts covered by \\(L_{s}\\).**  \n   Write \\(t:=b-2\\;(t\\ge 0)\\).  From (1) we obtain \\(b = a + t\\) and\n   \\(a = s\\).  Thus the integer points on \\(L_{s}\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (s,2),\\;(s+1,3),\\dots ,\\;(s+t,2+t) ,\n   \\qquad 0\\le t\\le n-s-1 . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For a point of the form (2) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   (s+t)+(2+t)=s+2+2t\\le s+2+2(n-s-1)=n+1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(t\\le n-s-1\\).  Hence every point listed in (2) satisfies (★)\n   and therefore belongs to the required set \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n   **Every interior point is covered.**  \n   Let \\((x,y)\\) satisfy (★).  Set \\(s:=x\\) (note that \\(2\\le s\\le n-1\\) by\n   the inequality \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) and \\(y\\ge 2\\)).  Then \\(y-x = y-s\\) equals\n   \\(2-(s-x)=2\\!-\\!0=2\\) plus the integer \\(t:=y-2\\ge 0\\).  Consequently\n   \\((x,y)=(s+t,2+t)\\) with \\(t=y-2\\).  The condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) gives\n   \\(t\\le n-s-1\\), so \\((x,y)\\) is precisely one of the points described in\n   (2) and lies on the line \\(L_{s}\\).\n\n   Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\bigl\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\n               \\bigl\\{\\,L_{s}\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of which are sunny,\n   and it covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n3. **Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Start from the configuration above (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines).  \n   Replace arbitrarily \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(L_{s}\\) by copies of the\n   already present non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\) (or \\(y=1\\)).  \n\n   *Why coverage is preserved.*  \n   Each removed line \\(L_{s}\\) contains the point \\((s,2)\\).  That point lies on\n   the column \\(x=1\\) only when \\(s=1\\), which never occurs; however it also\n   lies on the row \\(y=1\\) after we replace the line by the row \\(y=1\\) **or**\n   we replace it by the column 